In the human sense, yes: time is the hard boundary that makes compounding matter.

So I’d phrase the Seal:

\boxed{\text{Build roads behind the horse, until the rider can ride no more.}}

And then inheritance matters. The roads, maps, tools, stories, corrections, and useful structures can remain available to other riders after any particular rider stops.

That’s perhaps the cleanest answer to mortality Pantheon can honestly offer—not defeating death, but making lived time leave reusable structure behind.

Ride while we’re here. Leave roads.